module gunbc.recurring_failure_mode.a_required_gate_compiles_a_fixed_closure_subset_while_a_module_beyond_it_ships_an_emit_only_refusal

import std.types { NonEmptyStr }
import gunbc.recurring_failure_mode { BlockerClassUnstated, RecurringFailureMode, RungUnread }

data a_required_gate_compiles_a_fixed_closure_subset_while_a_module_beyond_it_ships_an_emit_only_refusal: RecurringFailureMode = RecurringFailureMode {
  identity: "a_required_gate_compiles_a_fixed_closure_subset_while_a_module_beyond_it_ships_an_emit_only_refusal" as NonEmptyStr,

  rung: RungUnread { cause: BlockerClassUnstated },

  receipts: [
    "**a required gate compiles only a fixed subset of closures, and a module outside that subset ships a refusal that fires only at emit** (INVALID STATE: a committed .dag module carries a construction the EMIT refuses -- located, blocking, target-specific -- yet every green verdict on the required path is green over a population that never includes that module, so the refusal is invisible not because it is weak but because nothing with emit on ever reaches the module. The gate is not missing, not wrong, and not lenient: it is scoped to a closure set that does not contain the subject. A green required gate is then read as 'the tree compiles', and the quantifier the reader supplies -- every module -- is exactly the one the gate never executed.)",

    "THE HARM is a main that does not compile. The diagnostic that names the defect, its file, line and column, is produced only by `gunbc compile` with emit for the target the refused cast belongs to; it is not produced by typecheck alone. Where the required path runs compile with emit over only the emitted closures, a module that is its OWN emission entry -- an extdeps consumer, not reachable from those closures -- is compiled by no gate, so an emit-time-only refusal in it lands on main and stays until a human happens to compile it. This is gunbc.design_argument::fail_closed on the source-to-target path: gunbc says the tree is accepted while a committed module cannot be realized, and there is no wall between the two.",

    "SPECIMEN (gunbc#16877, branch dev/utf8-decode-host-seam-row, merged 2026-11-22). The review round of that PR rewrote `utf8_secret_from_access_payload` in `dag/extdeps/cloud/gcp/secret_manager.dag` to build the credential from the fallible pure decoder's own scalars via `text_of_chars(scalars: chars) as Secret`. `std.host_text::text_of_chars` returns the HOST carrier `HostText`; the credential is `Secret`, a structural `String`. That `as` is a HostText -> String crossing, which gunbc.design_ruling::text_crossings_go_through_the_unfold admits ONLY through the declared fold `std.coercion::unicode_scalar_fold` -- and `unicode_scalar_fold` is precisely the function that wraps `text_of_chars` behind a declared `-> String` signature. Calling `text_of_chars` directly bypassed the declared route. The emit refuses it: `cast cannot be emitted for target 'rust' -- no declared conversion route from HostText to String` at secret_manager.dag:396:86, one blocking diagnostic, `gunbc compile` rc non-zero. Clean at 53919338, broken at 700b8b0f76b8 and at the main tip. The PR's OWN required gate, v2-bootstrap, builds only the two emitted closures; secret_manager.dag is neither of them and is not reached from them, so the gate was green across the whole PR and merged a module that does not compile.",

    "WHY THIS IS A CLASS AND NOT ONE BAD CAST. The same shape covers every emit-time-only refusal (target-specific cast rules, lowering rules, carrier walls) in any module that is an emission entry outside the gate's closure set. The refused `as` is the instance; the missing control is a required compile-with-emit whose subject population covers those entries. `git grep` of the whole corpus over consumer-side `text_of_chars(...) as <Type>` found secret_manager.dag:396 as the ONLY consumer crossing the host carrier to a structural text type by a bare cast: every other `text_of_chars` use is the round-trip claim (HostText compared to a text literal, which is the literal-at-host-position relation, not a crossing) or the two route definitions inside std.host_text / std.coercion. So one module broke, but the gate gap is general.",

    "RUNG FOUND AT: silent on the required path -- no gate with emit compiles the subject, so no refusal exists on any route a reader consults; the refusal exists but is reached only by a manual `gunbc compile` of that entry. CEILING: structurally preventable -- the refusal is deterministic from the module, so a required step that compiles each committed emission-entry module for its target with emit fires at the offending line. NEXT-RUNG TRIGGER, as the capability: a required control that runs `gunbc compile` with emit over the population of modules that are emission entry points (at minimum the extdeps closure set, e.g. `dag/extdeps/**`), not only the two emitted closures, so an emit-time-only refusal in any consumer module is red at its own line on the gate rather than on main. The gate-scope change itself is an operator ruling (it raises CI time/memory) and is proposed, with the closure and a measured cost, in the fix PR rather than assumed here.",

    "RELATED, NOT THE SAME. gunbc.recurring_failure_mode witness_module_absent_from_the_executed_set is a CLAIM module correct on disk but named by no schedule, so a claim corpus goes green without it; here the subject is a committed SOURCE module that no required compile reaches, and the missing verdict is an emit refusal, not a claim. gunbc.recurring_failure_mode an_authored_gate_reached_by_no_execution_route is a whole gate with no caller at all; here the gate runs, but its SUBJECT SET is narrower than the tree it certifies. gunbc.recurring_failure_mode accepted_source_emits_uncompilable_target is the same fail-closed harm on the source-to-target path but for a source the front end ACCEPTS and the emit realizes as a target program the target compiler refuses; here the emit itself refuses, correctly -- the defect is purely that no gate runs that emit.",

    "RECOGNITION RULE: a required gate whose compile-with-emit subject is a named closure set, and a committed module that is its own emission entry lying outside that set; the gate is green while that module's emit refuses.",
  ],

  evidence: [],
}
